Number = {7-8},
Pages = {1005-1011},
Month = {DEC},
Abstract = {In September 2006, Junichiro Koizumi fulfilled his term as president of
   the ruling Liberal Democratic Party (LDP). Shinzo Abe took over with
   overwhelming support within the LDP, and became the first Japanese prime
   minister born after the Second World War. Keeping on track the economic
   reforms begun by his predecessor, Abe also pledged to revise the
   pacifist constitution to give the military more freedom of action, take
   a hard line with North Korea and bolster the security alliance with the
   United States. Abe broke the diplomatic stalemate in Asia by visiting
   China and South Korea and resuming talks at the highest level to repair
   ties with the two countries, which had been damaged by Koizumi's annual
   visits to the Yasukuni Shrine dedicated to the nation's war dead.
   Although Abe made a remarkable international debut, his popularity among
   the public plummeted when the LDP allowed the rejoining of the Diet
   members who had left the party during an earlier row over postal
   privatization, and was further diminished by a series of scandals
   involving a minister and a top policy advisor. Less than a few months
   since the inauguration of the Abe Administration, many members of the
   ruling parties began expressing concern over the future of the
   Administration and asking whether their parties stood a chance of
   winning the nationwide elections in 2007.},
